## 12. Regulatory Framework & Grid-Arrival Risk

### 12.1 Regulatory Context

{{ site.site_name }} is located in {{ site.country }}, where the mini-grid regulatory framework is governed by the Nigerian Electricity Regulatory Commission (NERC). Key regulatory considerations:

- **Licensing:** Mini-Grid Regulation 2023 requires registration and licensing through NERC (or state regulators under the Electricity Act 2023)
- **Tariff regulation:** Developers can charge cost-reflective tariffs under the NERC Mini-Grid Regulation. NERC MYTO sets Band A tariff at NGN 225/kWh
- **Grid integration:** Tripartite agreement framework (developer, DisCo, community) for interconnected minigrids. DUOS charges apply for use of DisCo infrastructure
- **Land rights:** Site lease or community land agreement required
- **DARES program:** World Bank US$750M program supports mini-grid development through results-based financing via the Rural Electrification Agency (REA)
- **Embedded generation:** NERC mandates DisCos to target 10% of demand allocation through embedded generation by 2025

